ers between 0 (inclusive) and 5 (exclusive), call the ","key":"GlmGJ9dvJ0"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"set_seed()","key":"hjWOmqtsGI"},{"type":"text","value":" function and use ","key":"WzQuuaGLtA"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"42","key":"pm9NoNx2rm"},{"type":"text","value":" as the seed parameter.","key":"WyuAVMjvj3"}],"key":"xRxengukFW"}],"key":"ysL90q3htc"},{"type":"break","key":"sFcWpyfQTC"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Create a tensor “x” from the array above","key":"JKlTsFtAaS"}],"key":"MwliKD76uk"}],"key":"H7RpOoeHDs"},{"type":"break","key":"ryuzMoQMtU"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"5. Change the dtype of x from ","key":"iuxzKIcew4"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"int32","key":"IHd46zWhFE"},{"type":"text","value":" to ","key":"JWd91UMsp8"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"int64","key":"aacZVaonLj"}],"key":"ilLkhHZoPg"}],"key":"LvypuseKCu"},{"type":"break","key":"wC6xSwK4iP"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"6. Reshape ","key":"RYr6oiCauN"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"q7rR7agJRj"},{"type":"text","value":" into a 3x2 tensor","key":"R0PqbGmvvV"}],"key":"z53uLsvdId"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"tYvm1WUjJF"},{"type":"break","key":"auDPbNAAqD"},{"type":"text","value":"There are several ways to do this.","key":"iRM6IVRQHK"}],"key":"n4ED8uNjVb"},{"type":"break","key":"FNtJA3Kquz"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"7. Return the right-hand column of tensor ","key":"LWl1Ph5PKE"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"XV6nNCQw05"}],"key":"IhGDoZda1M"}],"key":"linfvzD8Rp"},{"type":"break","key":"SLUByZ6qjT"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"8. Without changing x, return a tensor of square values of ","key":"IANE3c4E3V"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"aA8glhuc0o"}],"key":"jdeG91wir4"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"sZNn8gDIo7"},{"type":"break","key":"KElIhJn1i2"},{"type":"text","value":"There are several ways to do this.","key":"nE1sHy5TgI"}],"key":"cJI8w8isUK"},{"type":"break","key":"l1pCszrChu"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"9. Create a tensor ","key":"T8XMMjKIU1"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y","key":"AYDeOOA9H9"},{"type":"text","value":" with the same number of elements as ","key":"It9FhzFozt"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"VFthpFst1Q"},{"type":"text","value":", that can be matrix-multiplied with ","key":"J6ve5ChJzA"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"oSLQYeJtTP"}],"key":"xIGZbAvOXK"},{"type":"text","value":" ","key":"V6UVRnDFaI"},{"type":"break","key":"osf9wEsmxs"},{"type":"text","value":"Use PyTorch directly (not NumPy) to create a tensor of random integers between 0 (inclusive) and 5 (exclusive). Use 42 as seed. ","key":"fbJj7YKgEI"},{"type":"break","key":"YZb9HfgP5J"},{"type":"text","value":"Think about what shape it should have to permit matrix multiplication.","key":"RKyGk1nUnE"}],"key":"Cf3CNfEUsq"},{"type":"break","key":"Q1NKvjLDaW"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"10. Find the matrix product of ","key":"sikbDWFiQo"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x","key":"l66wlrAvqJ"},{"type":"text","value":" and ","key":"ZJHzmFgV9c"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y","key":"FLFjgYbkvP"},{"type":"text","value":".","key":"W0VTT3Jjzw"}],"key":"MXCgaJ2ELr"}],"key":"tiOe998Qyj"}],"key":"zZPB15nA0R"}],"key":"jDq4LZsIIO"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Imports and Seed Initialization","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"uEpgu0n1Lz"}],"identifier":"id-1-imports-and-seed-initialization","label":"1. Imports and Seed Initialization","html_id":"id-1-imports-and-seed-initialization","implicit":true,"key":"YGoC77TpmN"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This section sets up the foundational libraries required for tensor operations and mathematical computations. The set_seed() function is defined to ensure reproducible results across both NumPy and PyTorch environments by fixing the random number generators using torch.manual_seed(). This guarantees that “random” tensor generation will yield the exact same values every time the code runs.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"KVSlvAkTXF"}],"key":"ZyijlwM8B9"}],"key":"WARdGVyX87"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 1. Perform Standard Imports\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\n\n# 2. Create a function called set_seed()\ndef set_seed(seed: int):\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    torch.manual_seed(seed)","key":"TkZC0pBJXx"},{"type":"outputs","id":"Y-v_gSbRM1vvgwpUFq_EG","children":[],"key":"vwcgBBJ1PI"}],"key":"Rs1Vhc3BWu"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. Array Generation, Tensor Conversion, and Reshaping","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"GJVxUIiwYh"}],"identifier":"id-2-array-generation-tensor-conversion-and-reshaping","label":"2. Array Generation, Tensor Conversion, and Reshaping","html_id":"id-2-array-generation-tensor-conversion-and-reshaping","implicit":true,"key":"W9B6f6UuS1"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here, a NumPy array of 6 random integers is generated and immediately converted into a PyTorch tensor using torch.from_numpy(). Because PyTorch operations strictly require matching datatypes, the .type() method is explicitly used to cast the tensor from int32 to a 64-bit integer (torch.int64). Finally, the flat tensor is transformed into a 2D matrix (3 rows and 2 columns) using .reshape(), which safely alters the tensor’s view without modifying the original memory block.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"QJo3XqWZJQ"}],"key":"SXSSFSJcY4"}],"key":"zAkNMkmVlW"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 3. Create a NumPy array \nset_seed(42)\narr = np.random.randint(0, 5, 6)\n\n# 4. Create a tensor \"x\" from the array above\nx = torch.from_numpy(arr)\n\n# 5. Change the dtype of x from int32 to int64\nx = x.type(torch.int64)\n\n# 6. Reshape x into a 3x2 tensor\nx = x.reshape(3, 2)","key":"AUreukwiL1"},{"type":"outputs","id":"q0F1uhNrw2zomzvKiTAm5","children":[],"key":"CsiuLjnDkA"}],"key":"aJ86GzY6wO"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"3. Tensor Slicing and Element-wise Operations","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"oYDREQnUfM"}],"identifier":"id-3-tensor-slicing-and-element-wise-operations","label":"3. Tensor Slicing and Element-wise Operations","html_id":"id-3-tensor-slicing-and-element-wise-operations","implicit":true,"key":"COLUgbSb0v"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This block demonstrates PyTorch’s native data manipulation capabilities. Extracting specific values from a tensor uses the exact same slicing syntax as NumPy arrays. The slice [:, 1:] grabs all rows (:) but only the elements from index 1 onwards (1:), effectively returning the right-hand column as a 2D slice. The .pow(2) function performs an element-wise arithmetic power operation, returning a brand new tensor containing the squared values without altering the original tensor x.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"wmeaikO5ty"}],"key":"ClhwSLe2jt"}],"key":"bgXuq8W0bg"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 7. Return the right-hand column of tensor x\nright_column = x[:, 1:] \n\n# 8. Without changing x, return a tensor of square values of x\nx_squared = x.pow(2)","key":"CvWOcYKDPn"},{"type":"outputs","id":"dB1PVhvua-q39YJv-udyE","children":[],"key":"rHXSm7xrT6"}],"key":"ILePxJLBaT"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"4. Matrix Creation and Multiplication","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"kfpjRtjWFV"}],"identifier":"id-4-matrix-creation-and-multiplication","label":"4. Matrix Creation and Multiplication","html_id":"id-4-matrix-creation-and-multiplication","implicit":true,"key":"KdPcSAQFzz"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To perform a 2D matrix multiplication, the number of columns in the first tensor must perfectly match the number of rows in the second tensor. Since tensor x has the shape (3, 2), the new tensor y is explicitly created from scratch using torch.randint() with an inverted shape of (2, 3). The operation ","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"SN18tv7Ulv"},{"type":"link","url":"http://torch.mm","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"torch.mm","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"WWYb4bwDub"}],"urlSource":"http://torch.mm","key":"rhYMV5JjPE"},{"type":"text","value":"(x, y) then calculates the matrix product.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"yQbust8t05"}],"key":"oWOPenGVzw"}],"key":"Z5ggHhhckD"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"# 9. Create a tensor y with the same number of elements as x, that can be matrix-multiplied with x\nset_seed(42)\ny = torch.randint(0, 5, (2, 3))\n\n# 10. Find the matrix product of x and y\nmatrix_prod = torch.mm(x, y) \n\n# Print statements to verify the outputs\nprint(\"x:\\n\", x)\nprint(\"\\nright_column:\\n\", right_column)\nprint(\"\\nx_squared:\\n\", x_squared)\nprint(\"\\ny:\\n\", y)\nprint(\"\\nMatrix Product (x * y):\\n\", matrix_prod)","key":"vPRRhVM3rI"},{"type":"outputs","id":"Z2ot9O1ewgvD1uV_9dNB1","children":[{"type":"output","jupyter_data":{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":"x:\n tensor([[3, 4],\n        [2, 4],\n        [4, 1]])\n\nright_column:\n tensor([[4],\n        [4],\n        [1]])\n\nx_squared:\n tensor([[ 9, 16],\n        [ 4, 16],\n        [16,  1]])\n\ny:\n tensor([[2, 2, 1],\n        [4, 1, 0]])\n\nMatrix Product (x * y):\n tensor([[22, 10,  3],\n        [20,  8,  2],\n        [12,  9,  4]])\n"},"children":[],"key":"G9V1iWnaAh"}],"key":"vb28vm1xl8"}],"key":"fPzTb8KaW0"},{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-content","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tensor Reshaping and Slicing Verification","position":{"start":{"line":1,"column":1},"end":{"line":1,"column":1}},"key":"JxmuSQ4wX1"}],"key":"i6zeVjqjIO"}],"key":"QVMVJflK0v"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The printed output of x confirms it was successfully reshaped into a 3x2 configuration. The right_column output [[4], [4], [1]] proves that the slicing operation [:, 1:] successfully extracted the final column while maintaining its proper 2D column-vector shape rather than flattening it into a 1D array.","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"tMZ8VXMLin"}],"key":"Z6PK2tqFbL"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Element-Wise Power Dynamics","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"G2MH0ZiCh3"}],"key":"xm3wyjDnhT"},{"type":"text","value":"\nThe x_squared output directly mirrors the shape of x, proving that basic arithmetic operations like pow() are applied on an element-by-element basis. Every individual scalar inside the matrix was multiplied by itself (e.g., the 3 became 9, the 4 became 16), entirely independently of the other elements in the tensor.   Dimensionality and the Matrix Product\nThe final output perfectly demonstrates the mathematical laws of matrix multiplication. Tensor x has a size of (3, 2) and tensor y has a size of (2, 3). Because their inner dimensions match (2 columns and 2 rows), the multiplication is valid. The resulting tensor natively adopts the outer dimensions of the two parent matrices, successfully outputting a new tensor with the expected shape of (3, 3).","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"jpuU1psIPB"}],"key":"vuuVyNKihU"}],"key":"CfcaZIlXUh"}],"key":"GiAXcQBonf"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}}}